Added OpenID Connect discovery, the JWKS endpoint, `id_token` minting and the userinfo endpoint — the point at which an off-the-shelf OIDC client can be pointed at this site's issuer URL and need nothing else. `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration` advertises the endpoints, the `jwks_uri`, the signing algorithm, the scopes and the claim list; every URL in it is built from the **configured issuer**, never the portal URL, because a conforming client compares the document's `issuer` to the URL it fetched the document from byte for byte and refuses the document when they differ. Deriving endpoints from `portal_url` would make that comparison depend on a proxy header, a virtual host or a trailing slash — exactly what configuring the issuer exists to be immune to. The document describes only what this server implements and the tests exercise: one response type, one algorithm, `S256` and nothing else for PKCE. An unconfigured server answers `503` with a named error rather than a traceback, because that is a site half set up rather than a bug in this package. The JWKS publishes every key in the ring, not only the signing one, so a relying party holding a token minted before the last rotation can still verify it by `kid`.

A token response now carries an `id_token` when — and only when — the `openid` scope was granted: a client that did not ask to be told who the user is does not get told, and an access token is a credential rather than an identity assertion. The `nonce` is carried from the authorization request through the authorization code and echoed verbatim in the `id_token`; this package's own client requires it as an essential claim with an exact value, so an authorization server that dropped it would produce federated logins that fail with nothing useful to say. The userinfo endpoint answers the same claims for clients that prefer to ask, with the scope read from the token so a caller cannot widen what it was granted. It validates the Bearer token itself and answers RFC 6750's `401` with `WWW-Authenticate` rather than relying on Plone's protection — which now redirects to a login page, correct for a browser and useless to a relying party's back-channel GET.

The gate check runs `authlib`'s OIDC client against the live server with nothing configured but the issuer URL: it fetches the discovery document, validates it with authlib's own metadata validator, fetches the advertised JWKS, completes an authorization-code flow with PKCE and a nonce, and validates the resulting `id_token` against the keys the document pointed at. @ericof
